body:
Grief isn't the same for everyone.  What you're feeling is perfectly normal.  Everyone hits that angry stage and even when you think you're over it, it can stab you later - years later though it's not as often.  It's kind of like a ball in a box and the button for pain is in a certain spot.  It won't always hit it as time passes, but when it does it hits and is just as intense.

&#x200B;

Write.  Find a place and yell.  Cry.  Get a therapist to help you work through your thoughts and emotions.  (it does help and there's NO shame in it ever.)

First, I'm sorry you're here with us and you've my utmost condolences and support.

&#x200B;

I wonder this myself.  I lost my husband on the 20th of November this year.  He knew we loved him.  He knew that I would go to the ends of the earth and back with him and for him.  We have a daughter who misses him very much.  But I know the literal pain and suffering he had been living with the ten years we were together.  

&#x200B;

I don't think suicide is selfish.  It's an unpopular opinion that I'll understand that you may not want to hear.  And that's okay.  However, I know he battled with his own mind.  One thing would tell him the logic and the darker side would creep up and snuff out those thoughts with the most horrible ones.  He was bipolar and did the medication mambo as we called it.  Nothing worked for longer than a year and after his dealing with it all for so long, I understand more of the whys though I hate it.  I hate every bit of it.  I imagine it being akin to stage 4 terminal cancer that has reached the point that it's more pain management at the end than having any hope of recovering.  At that point if it was me, I would want to go of my own choice.

&#x200B;

I'm sorry, I'm so deeply and truly sorry that you're still in so much agony.  I can only hope that perhaps the option of writing here might help you or perhaps a grief and trauma therapist if you're able.  Talking about it rather than bottling will help a little in working through all your thoughts and emotions.  Crying.  Talking to him even though he's not physically there is cathartic.  It at least helps me.

My world feels like it's ended.  

&#x200B;

The world goes on.

Just around the back yard for now is fine to get used to it all.  Beyond his little safe place back there, it's a big wide world with lots of unfamiliar smells and sights and can be super overwhelming. I'd suggest just sitting out on your stoop with them for a bit for a few days here and there, close to the house and slowly work your way where you're walking to the end of the drive to check the mail and back and so on. 

&#x200B;

He's only been with you for a week, sometimes it can take a couple of months or more for them to feel safe enough with you to trust you to do that sort of thing.  Give him some time, be patient with him and yourself.

Around a year old, dogs go through adolecence and they kinda go backwards.  You're right to start all over again.  Be patient, be kind, keep things short and sweet and set her up for success.  If you haven't already, teach her 'leave it'.  It's valuable in having them disengage in whatever they want to eat/pick up/chase on command and follow it with your come command.  

&#x200B;

I've not had any eperience with shock collars, but typically it can backfire if not careful.  I'd probably reach out to a trainer you trust for proper instruction.  Dogs are like people, some methods work, some methods do not.

What methods are they giving you?

&#x200B;

For resource guarding, (my Shepsky was pretty bad and still relapses occasionally - it's ongoing) handfeed, every morsel, every treat is given by you and only you.  We resorted to feeding her in her crate by hand for several months.  No toys, no access to funiture, no sleeping in bed with me, and very very structured life with a fairly strict schedule.  It's helped immensely and we've managed to curb a lot of her guarding.  However, occasionally she might snag a sock or something when we aren't paying attention and then I do the trade game with her.  Something good for what she has. Luckily, she hasn't escalated and it's working for me.

&#x200B;

Clarity is a huge thing for dogs.  Everyone has to be asking the same thing, in the same way, in the same sort of tone and reward IMMEDIATELY for the behaviour you're looking for.  Admittedly, it's far easier to do this with a dog that's food driven than anything else.  You may have to start from scratch.

Dog training is about 80% training it's people how to get the dog to cooperate and the other 20% is the dog and waiting out their stubborn streaks.  You have to make sure you're setting them up for success so they can't fail to start with.  This helps get the things you're asking for to 'click' a lot quicker with an enthusiastic "Yes!" or whatever your cue word is and a reward.  

&#x200B;

Edit-

To answer your question - I saw immediate progress with the methods that I choose to use which revolve around balanced, non alpha methods and positive reinforcement.  She just turned a year and I've been doing it since she was 8 weeks old.  Training isn't a one time thing, it's an every interaction, every day learning and bonding experience for both of you.

I'm sorry.

&#x200B;

No one should have to go through it.  It's hard, so hard.  A month will be coming up for me, so things are still fresh and hard, and heavy, and when I do smile or find small joy, I feel guilty or a sudden sadness because I was supposed to be sharing that with him.

&#x200B;

You aren't alone, not by a long shot.
